{{Japanese name|Sengoku}}

[[Yoshito Sengoku]] (仙谷 由人, Sengoku Yoshito; born 1946) is a Sanese politician who served as the 48th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from 2003 to 2006. A veteran power-broker within the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), his premiership was defined by a series of severe domestic crises, navigating the devastating 2004 Ise Prefecture Earthquakes before his administration collapsed under the weight of widespread corruption and cultural scandals.

==Early life and education==
[[Yoshito Sengoku]] was born in 1946 in [[Tochigi Prefecture]]. Raised in a family of provincial civil servants, he attended [[Okami University]], graduating with a degree in Political Science in 1968. Following his graduation, he worked as a political secretary for a prominent LDP Diet member, learning the intricacies of factional negotiations and campaign financing during the height of the Federal Republic's economic boom.

==Political career==
Sengoku was first elected to the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] in 1983, representing the [[Tochigi 2nd District]]. Throughout the 1990s, he built a reputation as a reliable and pragmatic backbencher, largely avoiding the spotlight while managing internal party logistics. 

During the premiership of [[Katsuya Okada]] (1998–2003), Sengoku served as the LDP's Secretary-General. In this role, he was instrumental in maintaining the fragile LDP-ONP (Okaiken Shintō) coalition during a period of painful economic restructuring and banking reforms. When Okada stepped down in early 2003 due to reform fatigue and low polling numbers, party elders selected Sengoku as a "safe, steady hand" to stabilize the government.

==Prime Minister (2003–2006)==
Sengoku formally took office as Prime Minister on 8 March 2003. He pledged a "return to fundamentals," promising to ease the aggressive economic reforms of his predecessor while focusing on public works and rural development.

===Ise Prefecture Earthquakes===
The defining crisis of Sengoku's administration occurred in the late summer of 2004, when a series of massive tremors, known collectively as the Ise Prefecture Earthquakes, struck Okaiken's central coastline. The disaster leveled several municipalities, displaced tens of thousands of residents, and severely damaged regional infrastructure.

Sengoku immediately declared a state of national emergency and mobilized the military for search and rescue operations. While his initial rapid response was praised, his administration soon faced intense public criticism over bureaucratic delays in distributing federal relief funds to the survivors. The slow pace of the reconstruction effort in Ise heavily damaged his reputation as a competent administrator.

===Scandals and Resignation===
By 2005, Sengoku's government was engulfed in controversy. A sweeping investigative report by the Sanese press revealed a massive match-fixing syndicate operating within professional sumo, the nation's highly subsidized and culturally revered sport. The investigation uncovered illegal gambling rings with direct ties to several LDP Diet members and bureaucrats who had accepted bribes to look the other way. The cultural shock of the sumo scandal deeply eroded public trust in the state's regulatory agencies.

The final blow to the administration came in early 2006. Federal investigators uncovered a kickback scheme within Sengoku's own cabinet, revealing that the Minister of Infrastructure had been funneling lucrative earthquake reconstruction contracts in Ise to a construction firm tied to the LDP's primary fundraising apparatus. 

Facing an imminent vote of no-confidence from the opposition and a rebellion from the ONP coalition partners, Sengoku resigned in disgrace on 8 March 2006, exactly three years after taking office. His sudden departure created a power vacuum that led to the unexpected election of [[Kenshin Hirota]].

==Legacy==
Sengoku's premiership is largely viewed by historians as a period of institutional failure. While he successfully maintained the LDP's grip on power following the economic stagnation of the 1990s, his inability to manage the Ise reconstruction and the rampant corruption within his cabinet cemented his legacy as a transitional, scandal-plagued leader. He remained in the Diet as a backbencher until his retirement from politics in 2012.

